{"version":1,"ops":[{"type":5,"author":{"id":"bfb0c87a087f143d5d4fb3c3b303347e581f98d0"},"timestamp":1491502230,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOkxhYmVsZWRFdmVudDEwMzI3MTA4MDM="},"added":["conference agenda item"],"removed":[]},{"type":3,"author":{"id":"bfb0c87a087f143d5d4fb3c3b303347e581f98d0"},"timestamp":1520528748,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDM3MTU1MzM4NA==","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/1454#issuecomment-371553384"},"message":"apparently still the case with 0.9.2\n```shell\n$\u003e datalad create /tmp/ddd; cd /tmp/ddd; touch .file; datalad add .\n[INFO   ] Creating a new annex repo at /tmp/ddd \ncreate(ok): /tmp/ddd (dataset)                                                   \nadd(notneeded): /tmp/ddd (dataset) [nothing to add from /tmp/ddd]\nsave(notneeded): /tmp/ddd (dataset)\naction summary:\n  add (notneeded: 1)\n  save (notneeded: 1)\n1 15119.....................................:Thu 08 Mar 2018 12:05:07 PM EST:.\n(git-annex)hopa:/tmp/ddd[master]\n$\u003e git status\nOn branch master\nUntracked files:\n  (use \"git add \u003cfile\u003e...\" to include in what will be committed)\n\n\t.file\n```\n@datalad/developers - what do you think?","files":null},{"type":5,"author":{"id":"cf7a0bdf96b5195bf3052274368f463c7410f7a8"},"timestamp":1523026603,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDE0OlVubGFiZWxlZEV2ZW50MTU2MTA5Mzc5Nw=="},"added":[],"removed":["conference agenda item"]},{"type":3,"author":{"id":"819663238374c863923453c36b916c251f565216"},"timestamp":1533567011,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDQxMDczNDcyMw==","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/1454#issuecomment-410734723"},"message":"Since this surfaced again, I'd like to point out, that I prefer the current behavior over the suggestion. After all - why would I \"hide\" something and simultaneously expect it to be found by some general tool like datalad? Doesn't make much sense to me. As long as it is possible to explicitly add/save it, it's fine as is, I think.","files":null},{"type":3,"author":{"id":"607ac2a9afabc0e44debe80e34283e413e009770"},"timestamp":1533567322,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDQxMDczNjUxOA==","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/1454#issuecomment-410736518"},"message":"@bpoldrack, FWIW, I prefer the current behavior as well.","files":null},{"type":3,"author":{"id":"e6d4813f22ddb02ab4ff047e0d9770a56e42fd25"},"timestamp":1533579001,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDQxMDgwMDEwNQ==","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/1454#issuecomment-410800105"},"message":"@bpoldrack Here is actually a situation I am facing regarding hidden folders as described in https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/274. Via a RESTful API, users can launch scientific pipelines executions on the server. Once the executions are done, the standard output and standard error are stored in a hidden folder within an execution specific folder on the server. This folder is hidden as we do not want to expose these files directly to our users (users can list and obtain their executions generated files through the API). However, via some specific endpoints, users can retrieve these files content so we need them to be published. Usually, when a pipeline execution completes, we manually save and publish the execution folder which in turn contains the hidden folder. However, we also have to manually publish the hidden folder. This is not too big of a deal for as long as things run smoothly. \n\nOn the other hand, as a fail safe, when starting the server, to insure all data is in sync, we save and publish the dataset. As hidden folders are not saved automatically, this forces us to manually explore our dataset to explicitly save the different hidden folders. Having an option to specify the inclusion of hidden folders on save would be a great addition. We would only perform one save for our execution folder and would be able to save our dataset without having to manually save each hidden folder.","files":null},{"type":3,"author":{"id":"e6d4813f22ddb02ab4ff047e0d9770a56e42fd25"},"timestamp":1533580153,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDQxMDgwNjMzMA==","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/1454#issuecomment-410806330"},"message":"Additionally, I just tested the following case with unexpected result. With the following file structure:\n```\ndataset\n└───.hidden\n│   │   file1.txt\n│   └───.hidden-child\n│       │   file2.txt\n```\nSaving .hidden saved both `file1.txt` and `file2.txt`.\n\nHowever, with this file structure:\n```\ndataset\n└───folder\n│   │   file1.txt\n│   └───.hidden\n│       │   file2.txt\n```\nSaving `folder` resulted only in the saving of `file1.txt`. \nIs this the expected result?","files":null},{"type":3,"author":{"id":"607ac2a9afabc0e44debe80e34283e413e009770"},"timestamp":1533580697,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDQxMDgwOTQxOQ==","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/1454#issuecomment-410809419"},"message":"\u003e Is this the expected result?\n\nHmm, I didn't know what to expect.  It does match `git annex add`s behavior, though, so it's expected in that sense.","files":null},{"type":3,"author":{"id":"819663238374c863923453c36b916c251f565216"},"timestamp":1533641408,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDQxMTAyNDgyMA==","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/1454#issuecomment-411024820"},"message":"\u003e Is this the expected result?\n\nI agree, this is a somewhat strange result and IIRC we actually can and should do sth about it to have it more straightforward. Thanks for pointing that one out!\n\nAs for your usecase @simon-dube: Generally, I get why you hide those folders and that you want to save and publish them anyway. However, I'd imagine there has to be some process creating those folders, so those processes know what exactly to add to the dataset without the need to discover it after the fact. So at some point in the workflow there should be a way to reasonably call and explicit `datalad-add` and/or `datalad-save`.\n\nNonetheless: I'm fine with an option like `--include-dot-files` as @yarikoptic proposed. That's just another way to explicitly state what is to be saved or added. My concern is about the paradigm. While I often don't really care what's the default as long as I can achieve everything, this is one of the cases where I think the default matters. And I'm strongly voting in favor of a default behavior that respects that things were \"hidden\" for a reason and not just treat it as if the leading dot has no meaning whatsoever.","files":null},{"type":6,"author":{"id":"819663238374c863923453c36b916c251f565216"},"timestamp":1533641408,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDE1OlVzZXJDb250ZW50RWRpdDExNjgzNzM4MA=="},"target":"080c068b79403333d84896a5d6d03cc5d489e57b9cce5b5e3907d6e413712ec7","message":"\u003e Is this the expected result?\n\nI agree, this is a somewhat strange result and IIRC we actually can and should do sth about it to have it more straightforward. Thanks for pointing that one out!\n\nAs for your usecase @simon-dube: Generally, I get why you hide those folders and that you want to save and publish them anyway. However, I'd imagine there has to be some process creating those folders, so those processes know what exactly to add to the dataset without the need to discover it after the fact. So at some point in the workflow there should be a way to reasonably call an explicit `datalad-add` and/or `datalad-save`.\n\nNonetheless: I'm fine with an option like `--include-dot-files` as @yarikoptic proposed. That's just another way to explicitly state what is to be saved or added. My concern is about the paradigm. While I often don't really care what's the default as long as I can achieve everything, this is one of the cases where I think the default matters. And I'm strongly voting in favor of a default behavior that respects that things were \"hidden\" for a reason and not just treat it as if the leading dot has no meaning whatsoever.","files":null},{"type":3,"author":{"id":"e6d4813f22ddb02ab4ff047e0d9770a56e42fd25"},"timestamp":1533655193,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDQxMTA5NDY5MA==","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/1454#issuecomment-411094690"},"message":"@bpoldrack In normal cases, I will be able to `datalad add` and `datalad save` the hidden folder manually. However, if the process is killed for any reason (e.g. power outage), I would like to insure that my server is in a safe state by saving the entire dataset without having to manually save all hidden folders.\n\nHowever, I also agree that the default behaviour should be as it is right now. It is true that someone would expect hidden files not to be automatically saved and published unless stated explicitely via a direct save or an option. Since Datalad builds on top of git-annex, it only makes sense for it to apply the same default behaviour. The addition of an option like `--include-dot-files` would however offer a nice flexibility for special use cases like mine.","files":null},{"type":3,"author":{"id":"cf7a0bdf96b5195bf3052274368f463c7410f7a8"},"timestamp":1540884428,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDQzNDE5NzczMQ==","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/1454#issuecomment-434197731"},"message":"I am on this now. My initial assessment is that I don't like the current behavior. The reason is that it makes a difference between a `GitRepo` and an `AnnexRepo` -- I don't see why we should make this difference, when in most other places we try hard to have them behave as similar as possible.\n\nThe key bit is this snippet in `AnnexRepo.add()`:\n```\n            if git:\n                # to maintain behaviour similar to git\n                options += ['--include-dotfiles']\n ```\n\nIf we would go for `--include-dot-files`, we should also make sure that this option is honored for GitRepo's, i.e. not include them if it is set to false. That is actually a rather tricky operation without the `.gitignore` mechanism.\n\nI would counter-propose to always include dotfiles unless they are .gitignored -- which is a configuration that we could add by default to newly created `AnnexRepo`'s. I am not at all confident that we should do the latter part. But relying on `.gitignore` seems like a rather familiar switch.","files":null},{"type":3,"author":{"id":"607ac2a9afabc0e44debe80e34283e413e009770"},"timestamp":1540905891,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDQzNDI5OTA3NQ==","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/1454#issuecomment-434299075"},"message":"\u003e The reason is that it makes a difference between a `GitRepo` and an `AnnexRepo` -- I don't see why we should make this difference, when in most other places we try hard to have them behave as similar as possible.\n\nThat's a good point.   While I prefer git-annex's behavior over git's, the most straightforward way to achieve consistency between git and git-annex does seem to be the \"always include dotfiles, use .gitignore\" approach.","files":null},{"type":5,"author":{"id":"cf7a0bdf96b5195bf3052274368f463c7410f7a8"},"timestamp":1541052940,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOkxhYmVsZWRFdmVudDE5Mzk3NTcwNjM="},"added":["fix-implemented"],"removed":[]},{"type":3,"author":{"id":"607ac2a9afabc0e44debe80e34283e413e009770"},"timestamp":1574111904,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDU1NTIxMzUyMw==","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/1454#issuecomment-555213523"},"message":"master now includes the behavior proposed and implemented by @mih above (i.e. call `annex add` with `--include-dotfiles` for consistency with git's behavior), so I think this can be closed.","files":null},{"type":4,"author":{"id":"607ac2a9afabc0e44debe80e34283e413e009770"},"timestamp":1574111904,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDExOkNsb3NlZEV2ZW50MjgwOTEzNDA2Mg=="},"status":2}]}